  npm test -s (runner-equivalent env)     exit 0, 1185 PASS, 0 FAIL
W-031's branch moves the pin to `seen === 31` (usage.test.ts:88). The M13
mutation is name-driven, not count-driven: it annotates a fixed list of 21
constants, so the scan drops from 31 to 7 and the assertion fails just as hard.
The mechanism is insensitive to the pin's value. No merge-order constraint
between PR #4 and PR #7 in either direction.

4. THE REDS
-----------
01.log — HONEST. Behaviour 1, `npm run -s format:check`, exit 1, header
tree:5d25eb5ab8e4a5dea910a8348fa59d66211a8d86. I recomputed that hash:
sourceTreeHash(repo, ["studio", ".bisellium", "examples/"], f119b41)
= 5d25eb5ab8e4a5dea910a8348fa59d66211a8d86 — the pre-fix master tree, so the
red is genuinely pre-implementation. Its body is the real prettier output
naming the real file. Here `format:check` IS the assertion, as the brief says.

02.log — HONEST BUT WEAK. Behaviour 2, run against a revert-stripped copy of
the script in a scratch repo, exit 1, header
dirty:5d25eb5ab8e4a5dea910a8348fa59d66211a8d86 (same pre-fix source tree,
dirty — correct, the mutation was on disk). Judged on the two things the
brief asks the behaviour to assert:

  (a) "tree clean afterwards" — the LOAD-BEARING half. Genuinely demonstrated.
      The deliberate break was deleting the `finally`/revert; the recorded
      consequence is "git status --porcelain after: DIRTY" followed by 15 ` M`
      lines that are exactly the 15 files in `targets`. That is the failure the
      mutation was designed to produce, it could not have appeared by accident,
      and I confirmed the converse myself in section 3 — the unbroken script
      leaves the tree clean on two different trees. The red bites on the
      mutation.

  (b) "exits non-zero" — CONTAMINATED. The scratch repo had no node_modules, so
      the script's inner `node --import tsx .../usage.test.ts` died with
      ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND ('tsx'). The recorded exit 1 came from that, not from
      the M13 assertion failing. This half of the red would have read the same
      against a correct script, a broken script, or an empty file. It proves
      nothing.

  VERDICT ON 02: advisory, not blocking. Three reasons.
  - The officina has already codified the answer. `opus.red_content` (P-005
    part 2, packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts:217-224) exists for exactly this
    shape and is `level: "advise"`, not "block". `check studio` on the branch
    raises it: 'opera/W-036.md  red(s) for behaviour(s) 2 are module-load
    failures, not assertion-level'. The PR does not evade the rule — it trips
    it, visibly, in the officina's own report. That is the system working.
  - The half that carries the behaviour's substance (a) is sound, and the QA
    lex §2 standard — one mutation is enough to demonstrate the suite bites —
    is met by it.
  - I ran behaviour 2's green side myself on two trees (section 3), so the
    behaviour is not resting on the red alone.

  INSTANCE, NOT CLASS. I searched all 115 red logs under studio/ci/reds/ for
  ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND / "Cannot find package" / MODULE_NOT_FOUND / ENOENT /
  "command not found". Two files match: W-036/02.log and W-022/03.log.
  W-022/03 is a false positive of MODULE_LOAD_RE — its body is a real
  assertion line, 'FAIL  ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND detected    false', where the
  substring is the assertion's NAME. So W-036/02 is the only genuine instance
  of an environment-caused red exit in the officina. Nothing to enumerate
  beyond it, and no fix should be dispatched at a "class" that has one member.

FINDING A (advisory, new, W-036's) — the caveat is not in the record.
The builder was honest about (b) above in its report to the orchestrator, and
that honesty is why this is a pass. But the report is transient. I grepped
every durable artifact the branch carries: studio/ci/reds/W-036/02.log's body
(raw stack trace only, no annotation), studio/opera/W-036.md, both commit
messages, the PR #7 body. None of them states that the exit code is not the
assertion's. A future reader of 02.log has to infer it from a stack trace.
Not blocking — `opus.red_content` now says it mechanically on every `check`,
which is a better home for it than prose. Recorded so the next round, or the
retro, does not rediscover it.

FINDING B (advisory, a class of 6, mostly pre-existing) — red provenance.
Both W-036 reds carry `# sella: guest`. That is $BISELLIUM_SELLA unset in the
builder's shell, not the builder's identity; the opus's traditio says
builder-a. Across 115 red logs: 82 builder-a, 22 builder-b, 6 guest. The six,
enumerated: W-028/{17,18,19,21}.log (pre-existing, W-028 still building) and
W-036/{01,02}.log (this PR). No check rule covers it — `bisellium red` accepts
whatever the env hands it. This is a candidate blocking rule, not a thing to
fix by hand in two files: an opus's reds should be attributed to a sella
declared in bisellium.yml. Raising it belongs in the cascade retro.

FINDING C (advisory, pre-existing, NOT W-036's to fix) — stale docstring in
the reformatted script. scripts/w030-usage-guard-mutation.mjs:30 says
"14 files, 21 constants"; `targets` lists 15 files (branch, close, docs, hooks,
instructions, new, providers, prune, retro, serve, tick, run, talk, verify,
writes) and 21 constants. The 21 is right, the 14 is wrong, and the red-02
evidence proves it — 15 ` M` lines. Line 4's "`seen === 30`" will also go stale
the moment W-031 merges (pin becomes 31; the mechanism survives, see section
3). Both predate this PR and W-036 is a reformat-only opus explicitly
forbidden to change the file's content, so correcting them here would have been
a finding in the other direction. Flagged for whoever next owns the script.

FINDING D (advisory, orchestrator's closeout) — `check studio` raises
process.checkpoint on HEAD: "last commit changed opera without updating
SESSION-HANDOFF.md or dossier progress". dcf0c2a is that commit. Pre-merge on a
branch this is closeout work, not builder work.

